 {
  "id": "cemc-fermat-1998-24",
  "bank": "cemc",
  "source": {
   "contest": "Fermat Contest (CEMC)",
   "paper": "1998 Fermat Contest (Grade 11)",
   "year": 1998,
   "number": 24,
   "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/1998/1998FermatContest.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "11",
  "points": 8,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "At some time between 9:30 and 10 o'clock the triangle determined by the minute hand and the hour hand is an isosceles triangle (see diagram). If the two equal angles in this triangle are each twice as large as the third angle, what is the time?",
  "image": "qimages/cemc/fermat-1998/q24.webp",
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "9:35"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "9:36"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "9:37"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "9:38"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "9:39"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "B",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Angle between the hands",
    "body": "If the third angle is $a$, then $a+2a+2a=180^\\circ$, so $a=36^\\circ$ and the equal angles are $72^\\circ$. The angle between the hands is one of the two equal angles (it cannot be the apex angle, since the two hands have different lengths), so it is $72^\\circ$.\n\nAt 9:00 the minute hand is $270^\\circ$ behind the hour hand (measured clockwise). The minute hand moves $6^\\circ$ per minute and the hour hand $\\frac12^\\circ$ per minute, so the minute hand gains $5\\frac12^\\circ$ per minute. The gap is $72^\\circ$ after $\\frac{270-72}{5.5}=36$ minutes: the time is 9:36.\n\n**Answer: (B) 9:36**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Check the positions",
    "body": "At 9:36 the minute hand points at $36\\times6^\\circ=216^\\circ$ (clockwise from 12) and the hour hand at $270^\\circ+36\\times\\frac12^\\circ=288^\\circ$. The angle between them is $72^\\circ$ ✓, matching the equal angles $72^\\circ,72^\\circ$ and third angle $36^\\circ$.\n\n**Answer: (B) 9:36**"
   }
  ],
