 {
  "id": "cemc-fermat-1998-25",
  "bank": "cemc",
  "source": {
   "contest": "Fermat Contest (CEMC)",
   "paper": "1998 Fermat Contest (Grade 11)",
   "year": 1998,
   "number": 25,
   "url": "https://cemc.uwaterloo.ca/sites/default/files/documents/1998/1998FermatContest.pdf"
  },
  "grade": "11",
  "points": 8,
  "timeMinutes": null,
  "type": "mc",
  "prompt": "For each value of $x$, $f(x)$ is defined to be the minimum value of the three numbers $2x+2$, $\\frac12x+1$ and $-\\frac34x+7$. What is the maximum value of $f(x)$?",
  "image": null,
  "choices": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "text": "$\\frac23$"
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "text": "2"
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "text": "$\\frac{17}{5}$"
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "text": "$\\frac{62}{11}$"
   },
   {
    "key": "E",
    "text": "7"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "Graph the lower envelope",
    "body": "$2x+2$ and $\\frac12x+1$ cross at $x=-\\frac23$; for $x>-\\frac23$ the line $\\frac12x+1$ is the smaller of the two increasing lines. $f$ increases until the decreasing line $-\\frac34x+7$ takes over, where $\\frac12x+1=-\\frac34x+7$, i.e. $\\frac54x=6$, $x=\\frac{24}{5}$. There $f=\\frac{12}{5}+1=\\frac{17}{5}$. To the right, $f$ follows the decreasing line; to the left, it is at most $\\frac12x+1<\\frac{17}5$. So the maximum is $\\frac{17}{5}$.\n\n**Answer: (C) $\frac{17}{5}$**"
   },
   {
    "title": "Combine the bounds",
    "body": "For every $x$, $f(x)\\le\\frac12x+1$ and $f(x)\\le-\\frac34x+7$. Taking $\\frac35$ of the first plus $\\frac25$ of the second removes $x$: $f(x)\\le\\frac35+\\frac{14}{5}=\\frac{17}{5}$. Equality holds at $x=\\frac{24}5$, where both are $\\frac{17}5$ and $2x+2=\\frac{58}{5}$ is larger.\n\n**Answer: (C) $\frac{17}{5}$**"
   }
  ],
